  },
  "sentences": [
   {
    "s": 0.0,
    "e": 2.3,
    "n": 11,
    "text": "Here are three animals that handle death just like humans do."
   },
   {
    "s": 2.38,
    "e": 2.94,
    "n": 3,
    "text": "First up, dolphins."
   },
   {
    "s": 3.24,
    "e": 4.26,
    "n": 5,
    "text": "For them, death means sinking."
   },
   {
    "s": 4.46,
    "e": 9.0,
    "n": 23,
    "text": "So, when a calf passes away, the mother will desperately try to push its body to the surface using her head or back."
   },
   {
    "s": 9.14,
    "e": 12.94,
    "n": 17,
    "text": "She keeps doing this until she's exhausted, often getting sunburned from staying above water for so long."
   },
   {
    "s": 13.02,
    "e": 16.14,
    "n": 15,
    "text": "Other dolphins will form a protective circle around the grieving mom to keep sharks away."
   },
   {
    "s": 16.22,
    "e": 18.66,
    "n": 12,
    "text": "Every dolphin has a unique signature whistle, kind of like a name."
   },
   {
    "s": 18.76,
    "e": 22.78,
    "n": 22,
    "text": "When one dies, the others will keep making that sound, as if they're calling out the name of the one they lost."
   },
   {
    "s": 22.96,
    "e": 23.48,
    "n": 2,
    "text": "Next, elephants."
   },
   {
    "s": 23.68,
    "e": 26.86,
    "n": 16,
    "text": "Besides humans, they are the only ones who can recognize the bones of their own kind."
   },
   {
    "s": 26.92,
    "e": 29.52,
    "n": 14,
    "text": "When an elephant dies, the whole herd stands guard over the body for days."
   },
   {
    "s": 29.52,
    "e": 32.56,
    "n": 14,
    "text": "They lower their ears, stay completely silent, and some even shed tears in grief."
   },
   {
    "s": 32.68,
    "e": 35.6,
    "n": 14,
    "text": "They eventually cover the body with leaves and dirt, a primitive form of burial."
   },
   {
    "s": 35.7,
    "e": 39.28,
    "n": 17,
    "text": "They even return to the site years later to gently touch the bones, remembering their lost friend."
   },
   {
    "s": 39.4,
    "e": 40.16,
    "n": 4,
    "text": "And what about crows?"
   },
   {
    "s": 40.26,
    "e": 42.68,
    "n": 14,
    "text": "When a crow finds a dead body, it lets out a specific alarm call."
   },
   {
    "s": 42.78,
    "e": 44.04,
    "n": 6,
    "text": "Within minutes, hundreds will gather around."
   },
   {
    "s": 44.16,
    "e": 47.56,
    "n": 18,
    "text": "After a burst of loud calling, the whole flock goes into a strange silence for about 30 minutes."
   },
   {
    "s": 47.66,
    "e": 51.36,
    "n": 18,
    "text": "Scientists believe they're actually studying how it died, so they can avoid that danger zone in the future."
   }
  ],
